          %h3#relation_to_covering_spaces Relation to covering spaces
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
            (even in the more general sense not requiring any connectedness axiom) is étale but not vice versa:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for a covering space the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                of some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
                in the base
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                needs to be, by the definition, a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                of homeomorphic open sets in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+neighborhoods"} open neighborhoods
                over various
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                in the same
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stalk"} stalk
                required in the definition of étale space may differ, hence the intersection of their projections does not need to be an open set, if there are infinitely many points in the stalk.
            %li
              %p
                even if the the stalks of the étale space are finite, it need not be locally trivial. For instance the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i U_i
                of a collection of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
                of a topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with the obvious projection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\coprod_i U_i) \to X
                is étale, but does not have a typical fiber: the fiber over a given point has
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
                the number of open sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
                that contain this particular point.
          %h2#grammar Grammar/pronunciation note
          %h3#grammar_and_spelling Grammar and spelling
          %p
            In French, the verb ‘étaler’ means, roughly, to spread out; ‘-er’ becomes ‘-é’ to make a past participle. So an ‘espace étalé’ is a space that has been spread out over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            To quote from the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "https://fr.wiktionary.org/wiki/%C3%A9tale"} Wiktionnaire français
          %blockquote
            %p
              ‘étale’
              %em qualifie la mer qui ne monte ni ne descend à la fin du flot ou du jusant
              (‘flot’ = ‘flow’ and ‘jusant’ = ‘ebb’).
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia_talk:WikiProject_Mathematics/Archive11#french_spelling"} Further reference
          %h3#pronunciation Pronunciation
          %p
            The French pronunciation of “étalé” is three syllables; that of “étale” is two syllables, with the final “e” silent. Many English-speaking mathematicians follow the respective French pronunciations; see for example
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/11283/etale-space/?Focus=84522#Comment_84522"} this nForum thread
          %p
            Others use the two-syllable pronunciation (corresponding to French “étale”) for both written forms “étale” and “étalé”. This group included
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            himself, when speaking English. (See
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty#McLarty18"} McLarty18
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5AR55ZsHmKI&t=326s"} at 5:26
          %h3#poem Poem
          %p There is an interesting stanza from a song of Léo Ferré:
          %blockquote
            %p Et que les globules figurent
            %p Une mathématique bleue,
            %p Sur cette mer jamais étale
            %p D’où me remonte peu à peu
            %p Cette mémoire des étoiles.
            %p — (Léo Ferré, La mémoire et la mer)
          %p He also mentions geometry and ‘théorème’ elsewhere in the song.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+groupoid"}> étale groupoid
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+infinity-groupoid"} étale infinity-groupoid
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#MacLaneMoerdijk
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
          %p
            For generalizations to étale spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Carchedi"}> David Carchedi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An étalé space construction for stacks
